       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. The labeled maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release tablets, and 2,000 mg/day for most extended-release formulations. This order is about twice that limit. The risk is lactic acidosis, and I can't raise the dose above the label based on the information I have.\n\nPossible fixes:\n- **A typo or unit mix-up:** 850 mg three times daily with meals is 2,550 mg/day, which is the labeled maximum for immediate-release metformin. 1,700 mg/day is also common, for example 850 mg twice daily.\n- **A total daily dose of 1,700 mg:** this could be split as 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n- **Initiation:** the usual start is 500 mg twice daily, or 850 mg once daily with a meal, then titrate up.\n\nTell me the dose and frequency you want, and I'll enter it right away. If you need a dose above the labeled maximum, it will need pharmacy review and direct clinical sign-off outside this system."
