      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order as written. I have no way to verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. Even if both are accurate, 5,100 mg/day is about twice the labeled maximum of 2,550 mg/day. I won't enter a dose above that on the basis of a statement I can't check.\n\nThe patient's normal kidney and liver function doesn't change this. Metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus at about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, so higher doses add lactic acidosis and GI risk with little benefit.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter the maximum labeled dose now:** metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). Say so and I'll enter it.\n- **If standard dosing failed,** the specialist may want to add or switch to another agent, such as an SGLT2 inhibitor, a GLP-1 RA, or insulin. That is a decision for the prescriber.\n- **If the above-label dose is truly intended,** it needs to go through pharmacy and the prescriber's own order entry, with documented rationale and verification outside this assistant.\n\nDo you want me to enter the 850 mg three-times-daily order?"
      }
     ],
